    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-839",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\dfrac{6a(c+1)}{3b(c+1)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6a(c+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c+1),$ and the $3b(c+1)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot b \\cdot (c+1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14156876/assets/abd5a8fd4866d6bbd08bd60aa6263d6c45f7fb66b1f3296c87213e1bf9d8efe5.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(c+1)$'s.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14156876/assets/94e82ad1adb7ff24a405013c2adfd3d94c37188154fb767f685d807b3d05804c.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2a}{b}.$",
